# SCANF(3S)

## NAME

scanf, fscanf, sscanf, vscanf, vfscanf, vsscanf - formatted input conversion

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int scanf(const char *format, ...);

int fscanf(FILE *stream, const char *format, ...);

int sscanf(const char *s, const char *format, ...);

#include <stdarg.h>

int vscanf(const char *format, va_list ap);

int vfscanf(FILE *stream, const char *format, va_list ap);

int vsscanf(const char *s, const char *format, va_list ap);
```

## DESCRIPTION

*Scanf* reads from the standard input stream *stdin*. *Fscanf* reads from the named input
*stream*. *Sscanf* reads from the character string *s*. Each function reads characters,
interprets them according to a format, and stores the results in its arguments. Each expects as
arguments a control string *format,* described below, and a set of *pointer* arguments
indicating where the converted input should be stored.

**Note:** the three *v* forms are C11 and take an already-started **va_list** in place of the
variable argument list. v7 had none of them.

The control string usually contains conversion specifications, which are used to direct
interpretation of input sequences. The control string may contain:

1.
: Blanks, tabs or newlines, which match optional white space in the input.

2.
: An ordinary character (not %) which must match the next character of the input stream.

3.
: Conversion specifications, consisting of the character **%**, an optional assignment
  suppressing character **\***, an optional numerical maximum field width, and a conversion
  character.

A conversion specification directs the conversion of the next input field; the result is placed
in the variable pointed to by the corresponding argument, unless assignment suppression was
indicated by **\***. An input field is defined as a string of non-space characters; it extends
to the next inappropriate character or until the field width, if specified, is exhausted.

The conversion character indicates the interpretation of the input field; the corresponding
pointer argument must usually be of a restricted type. The following conversion characters are
legal:

**%**
: a single `%` is expected in the input at this point; no assignment is done.

**d**
: a decimal integer is expected; the corresponding argument should be an integer pointer.

**o**
: an octal integer is expected; the corresponding argument should be a integer pointer.

**x**
: a hexadecimal integer is expected; the corresponding argument should be an integer pointer.

**s**
: a character string is expected; the corresponding argument should be a character pointer
  pointing to an array of characters large enough to accept the string and a terminating `\0`,
  which will be added. The input field is terminated by a space character or a newline.

**c**
: a character is expected; the corresponding argument should be a character pointer. The normal
  skip over space characters is suppressed in this case; to read the next non-space character,
  try `%1s`. If a field width is given, the corresponding argument should refer to a character
  array, and the indicated number of characters is read.

**e, f**
: a floating point number is expected; the next field is converted accordingly and stored through
  the corresponding argument, which should be a pointer to a *double*. **Note:** v7 said *float*,
  and distinguished the two. There is one floating type here. The input format for floating point
  numbers is an optionally signed string of digits possibly containing a decimal point, followed
  by an optional exponent field consisting of an E or e followed by an optionally signed integer.

**[**
: indicates a string not to be delimited by space characters. The left bracket is followed by a
  set of characters and a right bracket; the characters between the brackets define a set of
  characters making up the string. If the first character is not circumflex (^), the input field
  is all characters until the first character not in the set between the brackets; if the first
  character after the left bracket is ^, the input field is all characters until the first
  character which is in the remaining set of characters between the brackets. The corresponding
  argument must point to a character array.

**Note:** v7's page went on to explain that **d**, **o** and **x** may be capitalized or
preceded by **l** to mean a **long** pointer and by **h** to mean a **short** one, and that
**e** or **f** may be capitalized or preceded by **l** to mean **double** rather than
**float**. **None of that distinguishes anything here. short**, **int** and **long** are the
same one word, and so are **float** and **double**; **h**, **l** and a capital conversion
letter are still parsed and still mean nothing. v7's switch on the two attributes has two arms
here rather than six.

The *scanf* functions return the number of successfully matched and assigned input items. This
can be used to decide how many input items were found. The constant **EOF** is returned upon
end of input; note that this is different from 0, which means that no conversion was done; if
conversion was intended, it was frustrated by an inappropriate character in the input.

For example, the call

> | int i; double x; char name[50];
> | scanf( "%d%f%s", &i, &x, name);

with the input line

> 25 54.32E-1 thompson

will assign to *i* the value 25, *x* the value 5.432, and *name* will contain `thompson\0`. Or,

> | int i; double x; char name[50];
> | scanf("%2d%f%\*d%[1234567890]", &i, &x, name);

with input

> 56789 0123 56a72

will assign 56 to *i,* 789.0 to *x,* skip `0123`, and place the string `56\0` in *name*. The
next call to *getchar* will return `a`.

## SEE ALSO

atof(3), getc(3), printf(3), stdio(3)

## DIAGNOSTICS

The *scanf* functions return **EOF** on end of input, and a short count for missing or illegal
data items.

## BUGS

The success of literal matches and suppressed assignments is not directly determinable.

A scan containing a **%[** is not re-entrant: the character-class table is a file static and
**%[** writes on it. That is v7's bargain too, and is noted here only because it is easy to
forget.

## BESM-6 NOTES

### An argument is carried as a raw word

A **char \*** is a fat pointer - a byte offset above a word address - and an **int \*** is not,
so the two cannot share a C type without one of them being re-decorated on the way through, and
**a null that acquires the fat marker stops testing as null.** The engine reads each argument
with **va_arg(ap, int)** and reinterprets it at the point of use, exactly as the printf(3)
engine does for **%s**. v7 instead walked the caller's parameter block through an **int \*\***
handed in by *scanf*, which <stdarg.h> makes both unnecessary and impossible.

### Two v7 bugs are fixed here

The character-class table is indexed safely now. v7 wrote **_sctab[getc(iop)]** and only
afterwards asked whether the value was EOF - reading **_sctab[-1]** first. And its **%[**
walked a **char** subscript into a 128-entry table, which is out of bounds for any byte above
0177; **char** is unsigned here, so that is reachable.

